  "commit": "46a235bec6c09eb129186dab81eea509c95330ca",
  "description": "Written after its first merge rather than opened before it, which is the practice run/0020's closing note recovered: the history witness anchors on the newest run's last mergeSha, so an empty run is unrepresentable, and LOOP.md step 8 already said \"write docs/ledger/runs/NNNN.jsonld for the iteration\" -- a file that DESCRIBES an iteration, merges included.\n\nOPENING STATE, recorded so a later reader can check drift against something rather than against memory. main is 9939c6b, verified green per check name (tests 537, ledger 533, pages 27). No tags exist. No pull requests are open. The v1 GAP IS SIX: item/release-v0-1-0, item/docs-site, item/image-size, item/readme-owner, item/v1-audit, item/tag-v1-0-0. The gap is what this run reports, per decision/0105; the ratio may accompany it and never replaces it.\n\nNOTHING THE LOOP CAN ADVANCE. All three eligible items end at a decision only the owner can make, and each edge was probed rather than assumed: item/docs-site and item/release-v0-1-0 both turned out to have buildable halves, which shipped (PRs #274, #282, #286, #287); item/image-size was probed and has none -- image-size.sh's --print-only shares every measurement line with the gating call tests.yml already runs on every push, and release.yml's PULLED_TAG handoff between its two steps is correct as written. That third result is a finding, not a shrug: it is the first edge in this project that came back empty.\n\nFIVE DECISIONS WAIT ON THE OWNER.\n  1. Enable GitHub Pages (Settings -> Pages -> Source: GitHub Actions, then the DOCS_SITE_PAGES_ENABLED repository variable). Closes most of item/docs-site.\n  2. Authorise a v0.1.0-rc1 tag. Worth cutting now in a way it was not this morning: #282 made changelog-section.sh find a pre-release's notes, #286 made `latest` publish on a non-pre-release tag, and #287 built the three proofs the rc exists to produce. One rc would now count.\n  3. decision/0102's accounting question -- four groups of shipped surfaces the ledger does not track.\n  4. Whether `release` should be gated on the new boot jobs. A GitHub Release is the user-visible announcement, and announcing a version whose arm64 image did not start is worse than the image merely having been pushed; but the existing split was chosen deliberately, so changing it is the owner's.\n  5. Issue #289 -- about.version has no definition in context.jsonld, which blocks item/tag-v1-0-0 clause (4). Carried here because it was briefly held in a run/0021 description that then had to be deleted, and a note that lives only in a deleted file is a note that is gone.\n\nWHAT THIS RUN INHERITS AS METHOD, from the four corrections run/0020 closed on: report the gap rather than the ratio; an item born Completed says so in its own clause; both forms of validate-ledger run locally before any push, because only running the one with MAIN_SHA is how a tree CI refused was called sound; and a reassuring answer to a narrow question does not end an investigation (decision/0104).\n\nTOUCHED IS EMPTY ON PURPOSE, not by omission. This run's one merge is its own ledger PR; no v1 item moved. Listing item/release-v0-1-0 and item/tag-v1-0-0 there because the owner decisions below name them would have been the ratio mistake wearing a different field: a run that touched nothing reporting two items touched. If the owner unblocks one, it gets added when the work lands, not before.\n\nSECOND MERGE, 2f949c8 (#290). pullRequest null like the first: #290 is a ledger-branch PR, and the code it carried implements no item. It carried code because writing this run turned a test red. MainPushMergesRulesTest's recordedShasOfNewestRun() read the newest run unconditionally and asserted, in a comment, that 'the newest run always has more' than one merge; a run written for its first merge has exactly one, and a one-element list has no interior for a hole to be interior to. `guards` failed the same way through mutation/ledger-history-reports-unrecorded-merge, which reported it precisely -- the test 'did not pass with the guard in place, so it cannot prove anything about removing it'. The assumption was never true, only never tested at a run boundary: runs 0018, 0019 and 0020 were each created with merges = [], and the test was added at 1f5f6f8 when run/0020 already held 14. The assumption gave way, not the practice, because requiring two merges would mean a run could never be written at all. Verified by running LedgerValidator directly -- it is framework-free, so it needs no vendor/ -- against the real ledger: the old helper yields 1 sha and trips the precondition exactly as CI did, the new one yields 82 and passes all three legs.\n\nAND ONE THING THE LOOP COULD ADVANCE AFTER ALL, which this run's opening state said did not exist. item/release-v0-1-0's doneWhen still ended 'STILL NOT BUILT, and deliberately: the amd64/arm64 boot jobs this clause assigns to release.yml' -- twenty-four hours after PR #287 put all three of them on main. The correction was written into #287's merge commit message and the PR node's name, but not into the clause, which is the text the next run consults. Amended here, keeping the old paragraph rather than overwriting it, and recorded as decision/0106. The opening state was not wrong about the three eligible items ending at an owner decision; it was wrong that this left nothing to do.\n\nTHIRD MERGE, 26fadac (#291), main verified green per check name at it: tests 542, ledger 538, pages 32. That PR amended item/release-v0-1-0 and wrote decision/0106; this pass applies the same rule to item/tag-v1-0-0, which decision/0106 named in `affects` and which had never been amended since the consultation that wrote it, though #286 and #287 each implement part of it. Doing it in the pass that follows rather than filing it is the rule behaving as intended; a decision that prescribes an amendment and then does not make one is the same defect one level up.\n\nFOUR MORE MERGES, and the run's `touched` is no longer empty: item/tag-v1-0-0 clause (3) and item/readme-owner clause 1 were both discharged, by PRs #295 and #296. Neither item changed status -- each has a remaining clause that needs the tag or the published image -- which is the point decision/0107 records: a clause moved, the gap did not, and both are true at once.\n\n64d10e4 (#292) and 995cbc8 (#293) carry pullRequest: null for DIFFERENT reasons, and the second one is worth more than the convention it looks like. #292 is a ledger-branch PR, the usual case. #293 is not: it fixed CHANGELOG.md, which release.yml turns verbatim into a GitHub Release body, and no item names that surface -- so a PullRequest node for it would need an `implements` entry that does not exist, since the validator requires each one to resolve to an Action and all 26 existing nodes have a non-empty list. That is decision/0102's accounting question arriving as a concrete missing row rather than as an argument: a merged PR on the release critical path that the ledger cannot name.\n\nWHAT THIS PASS CHECKED BEFORE WRITING ITSELF, per decision/0107's own rule. Every open issue is accounted for: #294 and #289 are owner decisions, #115 and #109 were disposed with comments this run, #36 tracks post-v1, and #27/#30/#32/#33/#34/#35 are the six v1 items' own issues. The three eligible items' clauses were re-read individually; what remains in each needs a tag, a Pages setting or a published image. decision/0102's (a)-(d) were re-examined and deliberately left, for the reason set out in decision/0107.\n\nTWO MORE MERGES, both ledger-branch PRs and both pullRequest: null. #297 recorded four merges and wrote decision/0107; #298 corrected LOOP.md step 4 and wrote decision/0108, which found that 0107 had itself been recorded in breach of LOOP.md step 7 -- a decision requiring a change to that file changes it in the same commit or is not recorded yet, a rule carried since decision/0084 and now broken three times.\n\nTHE SWEEP CAME BACK EMPTY THIS PASS, and saying so is the point of decision/0107's rule rather than a formality. No tag exists, no repository variable has appeared, no issue has been answered, and main moved only by this loop's own two governance merges -- neither of which touches any v1 item's clauses. So every clause state is identical to the sweep recorded above, and re-deriving it would produce the same answer from the same inputs, which is not evidence of anything. The two merges recorded here are owed bookkeeping under item/ledger-main-push-record, not a reason the pass exists.\n\nTWENTY-ONE TICKS PASSED WITH NO CHANGE, and then the sweep found something it had been walking past. 46a235b (#299) is recorded here -- it was the trailing gap the validator permits, deliberately left because recording it alone would be a treadmill, and it now rides along with real work as that reasoning said it would.\n\nTHE SWEEP THAT FOUND IT was the same one the preceding ticks ran, with one difference: the open issue list was read as issues rather than as a list of things already accounted for. #109 and #115 had both been DISPOSED on 2026-09-20 -- folded onto clauses of item/api-content and item/api-presigned-upload, which is LOOP.md step 1's second option -- and every tick since read 'disposed' and moved on. Both items then completed and both issues stayed open, because nothing in the loop closes an issue that is not an item's own. The preceding ticks' sweeps were not wrong about the clauses; they never asked whether the ISSUES were still live.\n\n#115 IS CLOSED, correctly. StoreFileVersion::replace() exists at app/Actions/Files/StoreFileVersion.php:132, built by b7836ef under item/api-presigned-upload, with no File::create() in its body and no name argument for a caller to pass wrong; tests/Feature/StoreFileVersionReplaceTest.php covers it. The 2026-09-20 comment on that issue said the work was unbuilt and needed an owner-created item -- it was already present in the very commit that comment cites as the state it was read against, checked with git log -S and git show rather than recalled. A correction is posted on the issue.\n\n#109 WAS CLOSED IN ERROR AND REOPENED WITHIN THE HOUR. This is the pass's own defect and it is recorded as one. moveFile() and moveDirectory() were read, found scoped to viewableDirectoryIds() inside the query, and the whole issue was concluded discharged. It was not: selectDirectory() (Browser.php:270-291) holds the opposite posture on purpose and its comment names #109 as where the question belongs. Three call sites, two read, the product concluded from the two -- CLAUDE.md's 'counting readers is not counting sources' with call sites as the readers. The issue's own 2026-09-20 comment sets out both positions and says they cannot both be adopted, and it was not read before the close.\n\nTHE CLOSING EDIT ALSO OVERWROTE THE ISSUE BODY, which is the worse half. GitHub keeps prior revisions and they are reachable from the body's pencil menu, but no GraphQL tool is available in this environment to pull userContentEdits, so the body was reconstructed from the code and from that comment, labelled as a reconstruction, and readers are pointed at the edit history in preference to it. The two parts the comment identifies as stale -- a patch naming entry points that no longer exist in that shape, and a paragraph decision/0093 falsified -- were deliberately not reinstated as live claims.\n\ndecision/0109 records the rule both halves point at: folding an issue into a clause records where the work will be done and does not narrow what the issue asks, so an item completing means re-reading each folded issue against its OWN text and comments. LOOP.md step 1 changes in the same commit, per step 7. And item/api-content's doneWhen is amended: its claim that the posture was 'settled ONCE for the whole product' is false, and it is the sentence that made the wrong closure look supported.\n\nNO CODE CHANGED IN THIS PASS. The v1 gap is still six and the five owner decisions are unchanged.",
